  'homeschooling-bulgaria-samostoyatelna-forma': {
    cat:'homeschool', country:'bulgaria', featured:true,
    img:'linear-gradient(135deg,#0A1020,#8B1A2E)', splash:'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1601652114151-3d17a2e1eb1b?w=1200&q=75',
    t:'Homeschooling in Bulgaria: Самостоятелна Форма — the Commission That Grants It and the Oversight That Follows',
    date:'August 2026 · 10 min read',
    author:'Brendaliz Chelangat', role:'Head of Academics',
    metaTitle:'Homeschooling in Bulgaria 2026: Самостоятелна Форма, the РУО Commission & Annual Exams — Smartious',
    metaDesc:'Bulgaria allows independent study — but at a parent\u2019s request it is granted by a regional expert commission and supervised every school term. The complete guide to how it works, what it requires, and when a simpler route is better.',
    intro:'Bulgaria does allow a child to be educated outside the daily classroom, and the route has a name: самостоятелна форма на обучение, the independent form of education. What most summaries leave out is everything that follows the name — that at a parent\u2019s request it is granted by an expert commission at the Regional Education Directorate rather than claimed by notification, that the same commission supervises the examination sessions and their results every school term, and that progression through the grades is strictly sequential. This guide sets all of it out, and is candid about when a family is better served by a simpler arrangement entirely.',
    sections:[
      {h:'What the independent form actually is',p:'Under the Preschool and School Education Act, самостоятелна форма consists of two things: independent preparation by the student, and examinations organised by the school to determine annual grades in the subjects of the school curriculum. The day-form curriculum applies — this is not an alternative programme but the national one, assessed differently. The school publishes the examination schedule, provides consultation hours with teachers under a schedule approved by its pedagogical council, and students apply to sit each session roughly ten days in advance.'},
      {h:'Who it is organised for — three categories, not one',p:'The Act sets out distinct groups and they are frequently confused. First, students of compulsory school age who cannot study in the day form for health reasons, certified by the relevant medical expert commission — a welfare provision, and not a pathway anyone should be steered toward. Second, students of compulsory school age at the request of the student or parent under Article 12(2), following a decision of an expert commission established at the Regional Education Directorate. Third, students who have passed compulsory school age, who fall into a separate category with a lighter route. It is the middle one that most families mean when they ask about homeschooling in Bulgaria, and it is a permission rather than a right.'},
      {h:'The oversight, which deserves to be understood before applying',p:'For that parent-request category the Act provides that the РУО expert commission — which includes a representative of the Social Assistance Agency — exercises control each school term over the organisation and conduct of the examination sessions and over their results. Termly supervision by a body that includes social services is unusual among the frameworks we cover across Europe. It is not a reason to avoid the route, and families do use it successfully. It is a reason to go in with clear eyes: the relationship is with the Regional Education Directorate as well as the school, it recurs every term, and it is about outcomes as much as arrangements.'},
      {h:'Progression is sequential, and timing is fixed',p:'Two mechanical points shape the year more than families expect. Progression is strict: a student may sit examinations for the next grade only after successfully passing all the examinations required to complete the previous one, so a subject left unpassed does not simply carry — it blocks. And entry is a start-of-year decision: switching into the independent form mid-year is permitted for students turning sixteen in that year or where new circumstances arise, but not for the parent-request category. A family deciding in November is deciding for the following September. The detail lives in ministerial ordinances rather than the Act alone, so current requirements are confirmed with your school and your РУО.'},
      {h:'When we recommend something simpler — and we usually do',p:'Here is where our advice may differ from what a provider is expected to say. For most international, expat, and internationally mobile Bulgarian families, the better arrangement is not the independent form at all: keep the child in an ordinary day-form enrolment, which carries every legal obligation, requires no commission, brings no termly supervision, and leaves the family free — and run the Cambridge or IB track live alongside it in the after-school hours. Bulgaria sits only zero to one hours ahead of our teaching base, so that slot works comfortably. The child ends with the same externally examined qualifications and the family with none of the administrative exposure. The independent form earns its place where a family genuinely needs to step out of daily schooling; where they do not, it adds obligations without adding outcomes.'},
      {h:'After sixteen, and where it all leads',p:'Compulsory school age in Bulgaria ends at sixteen, and two things change. Inside the system, the independent form becomes available to students in their own right rather than through the parent-request commission route. Outside it, a student past compulsory school age carries no Bulgarian schooling obligation at all — which makes the A-Level years straightforward: two or three years of live teaching and sittings at authorised provision, with nothing else to satisfy. The destinations are wider than Bulgaria\u2019s size suggests: the medical universities at Sofia, Plovdiv, and Varna teach thousands of international students in English, the American University in Bulgaria runs a US-style liberal arts programme at Blagoevgrad, and both read Cambridge and IB qualifications directly — alongside UCAS reading A-Levels natively and German, Dutch, Austrian, and Romanian universities assessing them routinely.'},
    ],
    faqs:[
      {q:'Is homeschooling legal in Bulgaria?',a:'There is a lawful route — самостоятелна форма на обучение — consisting of independent preparation plus annual examinations in every subject of the school curriculum. At a parent\u2019s request for a student of compulsory school age it requires a decision by an expert commission at the Regional Education Directorate.'},
      {q:'What does the termly oversight involve?',a:'For the parent-request category the РУО commission, which includes a Social Assistance Agency representative, controls the organisation and conduct of the examination sessions and their results each school term.'},
      {q:'Why do you often recommend against it?',a:'Because for most families an ordinary day-form enrolment achieves the same academic outcome with none of the administrative exposure: it carries every legal obligation, needs no commission, and leaves the Cambridge or IB track free to run live alongside it after school.'},
      {q:'What changes at sixteen?',a:'Compulsory school age ends. The independent form becomes available to students in their own right, and a student outside the system has no Bulgarian schooling obligation at all — which is what makes the A-Level years straightforward.'},
    ],
    conclusion:'Bulgaria gives families a real route and supervises it closely, and the honest advice is to understand both before choosing — then, in most cases, to take the simpler path. Smartious explains the framework, plans whichever configuration fits, and teaches live through to A-Level. Book an assessment for your child, or start with our Bulgaria hub and city pages.',
  },

